<article><header><p class="eyebrow">Depression and mood / Later life</p><h1>Depression in older adults: signs, assessment and support</h1><p class="lead">Depression is not a normal part of ageing. In later life, changes in mood or interest may appear alongside tiredness, sleep problems, concentration changes or physical discomfort. These symptoms can also have other causes, so an individual assessment matters.</p><div class="blog-answer"><strong>In short:</strong> If an older adult has persistent low mood, loss of interest or changes affecting daily life, encourage a conversation with a doctor or qualified mental-health professional. Do not assume symptoms are “just ageing” or assume that physical symptoms are caused by depression.</div><p class="editorial-note">General education; not a diagnosis or treatment plan.</p></header>
<nav class="article-toc" aria-label="On this page"><strong>On this page</strong><ol><li><a href="#signs">Signs to notice</a></li><li><a href="#assessment">What an assessment may include</a></li><li><a href="#support">Treatment and support</a></li><li><a href="#family">How family can help</a></li><li><a href="#urgent">Urgent support in India</a></li><li><a href="#sources">Sources</a></li></ol></nav>
<section id="signs"><h2>Signs to notice</h2><p>Depression affects people differently. Some older adults may describe sadness; others may mainly notice emptiness, irritability, reduced interest, fatigue, changes in sleep or appetite, difficulty concentrating, or pulling away from usual activities. Some people also experience aches or digestive symptoms. A physical symptom deserves appropriate medical attention rather than an assumption about its cause.</p><p>A change from the person's usual mood, routines or functioning can be useful to describe to a clinician. One symptom alone does not establish depression.</p></section>
<section id="assessment"><h2>What an assessment may include</h2><p>A clinician may ask when changes began, how often they occur, and how they affect daily activities, relationships and sleep. They may review medical conditions, medicines, alcohol use, recent losses and earlier mental-health history. Depending on the situation, a physical examination or tests may be considered to check for other contributors.</p><p>Questionnaires can support a conversation but do not diagnose by themselves. Share concrete examples and a current medicine list if available. The older adult should be included in decisions as much as they wish and are able.</p></section>
<section id="support"><h2>Treatment and support</h2><p>Support is individual. It may include psychological therapy, medication, practical support, or a combination. A qualified clinician can discuss likely benefits, risks, other health conditions, medicine interactions, preferences and follow-up. Do not start, stop or change a medicine based on a webpage.</p><p>Regular routines, social contact and appropriate movement may support wellbeing, but they are not a replacement for an assessment when symptoms persist or cause distress. The National Institute on Aging notes that depression in older adults can be treated and that both counselling and medication may be considered.</p></section>
<section id="family"><h2>How family can help</h2><p>Choose a calm moment and describe what you have noticed without labelling the person: “You have seemed less interested in your usual walks and are sleeping poorly. Would you be open to discussing this with your doctor?” Offer help arranging or attending an appointment, while respecting the person's preferences.</p><p>If the person has difficulty travelling, ask their usual clinician about accessible or remote options. Do not hide medication, make a diagnosis on their behalf, or pressure them into a treatment decision.</p></section>
<section id="urgent"><h2>Urgent support in India</h2><p>If someone may act on thoughts of suicide, cannot stay safe, or is in immediate danger, call <a href="tel:112">112</a> or go to the nearest emergency department. For mental-health support, call the Government of India's Tele-MANAS helpline at <a href="tel:14416">14416</a> or <a href="tel:1800-89-14416">1800-89-14416</a>. See the <a href="/emergency">emergency resources page</a> for official links.</p></section>
<section id="sources"><h2>Sources and further reading</h2><ol><li><a href="https://www.nia.nih.gov/depression">National Institute on Aging: Depression and Older Adults</a>.</li><li><a href="https://www.nia.nih.gov/health/mental-and-emotional-health/depression-and-older-adults">National Institute on Aging: Depression and Older Adults—symptoms and treatment</a>.</li><li><a href="https://dghs.mohfw.gov.in/national-mental-health-programme.php">Government of India, Directorate General of Health Services: Tele-MANAS</a>.</li></ol><p>This page offers general information and does not provide an individual assessment. No named clinician review is claimed.</p></section></article>
